No `.csproj` found, so I'll give you the recommended approach directly.

## Lightest option: `TimeProvider` (built-in since .NET 8)

No new packages or interfaces needed — just accept `TimeProvider` as a constructor parameter:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**In production** — nothing changes, the default uses the real clock:
```csharp
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator();
```

**In tests** — pass `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:
```
dotnet add <TestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```
```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 5, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest approach:**
- `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL — zero new abstractions to write
- The optional parameter means no DI container is needed
- `FakeTimeProvider` gives you full control (set time, advance time)
- Works for `DateTime`, `DateTimeOffset`, and `Task.Delay`/`CancellationTokenSource` too

